  "limitations": [
    "72 tickets: one ticket is 1 percentage points. A gap of 3 points between two runs is two tickets - do not read it as a trend.",
    "NOT TESTED AT ALL, no ticket of this class in the dataset: urgency=critical, impact=enterprise. This run says nothing about them - a perfect score here is silent on these classes.",
    "Thin classes, fewer than 5 tickets each: category=erp (n=3), category=telecom (n=4), impact=site (n=3). Their per-class scores are anecdotes, not measurements. No file in this repo has enough rare-class tickets to prove rare-class quality - val.jsonl included.",
    "requested_action: It compares WORDS, not meaning, and the expected clauses are written in one house style. 'Reset password' vs 'Reset the user's MyPortal password' scores 0.33 though a desk agent would do the same thing; 'Reset the password' vs 'Do not reset the password' scores 0.60. Treat the match rate as a floor - it rewards house wording - and read the sample below the threshold (rubric H2).",
    "Labels follow written rules (corpus/README.md, 'Labelling rules'). Where a rule is arguable - mostly urgency - a 'miss' may be a defensible answer. Rubric H1 handles that.",
    "One run at temperature 0. Local models can still vary slightly between runs and machines; hosted models change under you. Re-run before quoting a number."
  ]
